A||N||7||bilbo||Oxygen sensor question?||||1||
Z||000000||bilbo||01-12-2001||12:32 PM||||The cheap guy that I am always trying too save a buck!! My 86 se needs a oxy-sensor just bought a y-pipe that had one looks good how will I know that it is??? Just another quick ?:my car has no oxy sensor right now just a hole where it should be,shouldn't this set off a trouble code?check engine light??? Thanks for the help!!!! ||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||01-12-2001||12:52 PM||||If there's no oxygen sensor plugged into the pigtail connector, then, yes, the ECM should set a code.  Maybe your car doesn't have a working "service engine soon light?||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||01-12-2001||01:09 PM||||Def should throw a code. O2 sensors arent that much..Id go on and get one. However, no major problems are gonna happen w/o one for a while...I ran my SE without one for a year...no biggie.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fiero Fanatic||01-12-2001||05:02 PM||||When I disconnected mine in my 84, It ran like total S**T . The way you test them is to get a torch and heat up the O2 sensor. It has to reach hotter than 600 degrees. As your heating it, test the pigtail with a volt meter. It should omit about a .45 volts every few seconds. If you put in the sensor the ECM should give you a code 13 if it's bad. A code 44 for lean exhaust and a code 45 if it's rich exhaust. Also just consider replacing it. They only cost about $25 dollars anyway.<p>[This message has been edited by Fiero Fanatic (edited 01-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Hartz||01-12-2001||06:12 PM||||I would second this - get a new O2 sensor.  I was STUNNED at how much better my car ran with a new O2 sensor after I replaced it.  I think I got mine at the local auto parts store for $20.  It's definately money well invested, IMHO.<P>Hartz<BR>'85 SE v6 Turbo||||reg||4
Z||000005||88formula||01-12-2001||08:45 PM||||I agree with Hartz 100%.||||reg||1
Z||000006||Eric||01-12-2001||09:42 PM||||You should run with an O2 sensor.  Mine got disconnected for about a week (was thinking the check engine light was for something else).  Then I started noticing pinging which wasn't from too low octane.  My air/fuel moniter was reading a perfect mixture all the time, but it should bounce between rich and lean constantly.  The engine wasn't getting enough fuel.||||reg||1
Z||000007||87GTZ34||01-12-2001||09:50 PM||||Bilbo,  That O2 sensor probably doesn't have 2K miles on it so I would try using it.  I had the receipt from the butcher that put the engine in the car before I bought it and the owner only put 1200 miles on it.  I only put 450 mi on it before it started to knock.  The emissions worked fine (before the knock) when I took the motor out.  You'll need to remove the sensor from the boss (don't use torches) and get a new boss welded in the pipe.<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 01-13-2001).]||||reg||1
